<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oshki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">963–<a href="/years/973">973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Öşkvank</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oshki is a medieval Georgian monastery in the historic region of Tao, today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> Province, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Its great domed church was built between 963 and 973 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iii-kuropalates">David III Kuropalates</a> and his brother Bagrat, and its walls carry long building inscriptions and relief carvings of the patrons. It is one of the finest monuments of the Tao-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a> school of architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oshki.jpg" alt="Oshki" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Inalo1989 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oshki1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
